8. What I Learned

The biggest engineering lesson from this project was that accessibility has to be tested physically, not just described as an intention. In the first version, the sign looked clear enough as an object, but the tactile features and visibility were not strong enough. That showed that a design can meet one goal while still failing another.

This project also changed how I understand iteration. Before this class, I usually cared most about the final result when I was crafting something. This semester showed me that the methodology is just as important: documenting the process, testing the design, and explaining why each change happened. Each cycle focused on a different type of design change: first the basic form, then CAD geometry, then CAM settings, then material. That made the process more systematic. Instead of randomly changing the whole prototype, we could connect each change to a specific test result.

I also learned that small technical choices can strongly affect user experience. The height of tactile icons, the placement of zip-tie holes, the engraving settings, the vinyl contrast, and the material surface all changed whether the hub was visible, stable, and usable.

Another learning outcome I feel proud of is the website itself. Working on the site throughout the class forced me to look back at my work, organize it, and improve it bit by bit. The website became more flexible and easier to update than I expected, and it helped me practice communicating engineering work clearly.

9. If I Had More Time

If we had more time, I would improve the design through more real-world testing. The next version should be tested outdoors over a longer period, including rain, snow, night conditions, and repeated use by different people. I would also want feedback from students who actually use mobility aids or have low vision, because they are the target users.

Future improvements could include:

- stronger weatherproof mounting
- more durable outdoor materials
- improved lighting or reflective elements
- clearer tactile symbols
- a more standardized labeling or sorting system for multiple deliveries
- more testing with delivery drivers using the real GPS pin
- long-term checks for acrylic scratching, glare, or loosening zip ties
- more user testing to confirm whether the 20.6-foot visibility result holds across different locations and lighting conditions

On a process level, I would also want the final project to have more time for slower iteration. Compared with the earlier CS1103 software engineering project, where we had the whole semester to learn, practice, and improve with teammates, this final project felt more compressed. More time would have allowed us to take the methodology more seriously and complete deeper testing before the final version.

10. Final Reflection / Conclusion

The most valuable part of this project was seeing how the design improved because of testing. The final acrylic hub was not just a nicer-looking version of the first sign; it was the result of specific problems we found in each cycle. The project started with a broad accessibility problem and became a physical object with measurable performance goals.

My biggest personal growth was learning to think about engineering decisions as tradeoffs between users, materials, manufacturing, and testing. I came into the semester wanting to learn and experience engineering, and possibly explore whether I was interested in an engineering career. This project helped me understand that engineering is not only making a final object; it is also about defining the problem, documenting the process, testing assumptions, and improving the design based on evidence.

A successful prototype is not only the one that looks finished. It has to work for the actual user, in the actual environment, under the constraints of available tools and materials. This project made me more aware that good design depends on both the final product and the reasoning process behind it.

The open-source guide was also an important final outcome. By including price listings, step-by-step directions for shelf assembly, and digital files for laser-cut signage in acrylic or plywood, the project became more scalable. The goal was not just to solve one delivery problem once, but to create a kit that other people could remake, adapt, and improve.
